WEBVTT
TITLE Matrices, Systems and Eigenvalues — GATE Visual Explainer
NOTE Timings are estimated from the lesson timeline (speaking-rate based), not measured from recorded audio.

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.500
A matrix is not a table of numbers. It is a function that takes a vector in and gives a vector out.

2
00:00:07.500 --> 00:00:15.000
Ax mixes the columns of A: each entry of x says how much of that column to take.

3
00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:22.500
Multiplication is composition: AB means apply B first, then A.

4
00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:30.000
Shape tells you what can happen: an m by n matrix cannot have rank above the smaller of m and n.

5
00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:37.500
The determinant is the factor by which the map scales area — or volume, in higher dimensions.

6
00:00:37.500 --> 00:00:45.000
For the two by two with rows 2,1 and 1,3 the determinant is 5, so areas are multiplied by five.

7
00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:52.500
A negative determinant means the map flips orientation; a zero determinant means it flattens space.

8
00:00:52.500 --> 00:01:00.000
Determinants multiply, and a row swap negates them. Both facts are asked directly.

9
00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:07.500
Rank is the number of independent directions the columns actually span.

10
00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:15.000
Row reduction finds the rank: count the pivots and you have counted the rank.

11
00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:22.500
Rank plus nullity equals the number of columns. The nullity counts the directions sent to zero.

12
00:01:22.500 --> 00:01:30.000
The three by three with rows 1 to 9 has rank two and nullity one, so its determinant is zero.

13
00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:37.500
Ax = b asks which combination of the columns of A produces b.

14
00:01:37.500 --> 00:01:45.000
Compare rank(A) with rank of the augmented matrix: equal means consistent, larger means no solution.

15
00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:52.500
Consistent with rank below the number of unknowns means free variables: infinitely many solutions.

16
00:01:52.500 --> 00:02:00.000
For a wide matrix with m greater than n, the homogeneous system has at least m minus n independent solutions.

17
00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:07.500
Elimination can be written down as a product: A equals L times U.

18
00:02:07.500 --> 00:02:15.000
Plain LU exists when the leading pivots are all non-zero — the leading principal minors decide.

19
00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:22.500
When a pivot is zero, swap rows: then P A equals L U, with P a permutation matrix.

20
00:02:22.500 --> 00:02:30.000
Then solve twice: forward for y in Ly = b, and back for x in Ux = y. Each pass is cheap.

21
00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:37.500
An eigenvector is a direction the map does not turn; it only stretches or flips it.

22
00:02:37.500 --> 00:02:45.000
The stretch factor is the eigenvalue: A v equals lambda v, with v not the zero vector.

23
00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:52.500
Find them from det(A minus lambda I) equals zero: the characteristic polynomial.

24
00:02:52.500 --> 00:03:00.000
Trace is the sum of the eigenvalues and determinant is their product. Two free checks on any answer.

25
00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:07.500
A triangular matrix has its eigenvalues written on the diagonal, with no work at all.

26
00:03:07.500 --> 00:03:15.000
All ones minus the identity has eigenvalues n minus one, and minus one repeated n minus one times.

27
00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:22.500
Powers and inverses act on the eigenvalue alone: A to the k has lambda to the k, and A inverse has one over lambda.

28
00:03:22.500 --> 00:03:30.000
A real matrix can have no real eigenvalue: a quarter turn sends every vector to a perpendicular one.

29
00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:37.500
Diagonalising means writing A as P D P inverse, with D holding the eigenvalues.

30
00:03:37.500 --> 00:03:45.000
A repeated eigenvalue may fail to supply enough eigenvectors, and then the matrix is defective.

31
00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:52.500
Symmetric matrices always succeed, with real eigenvalues and perpendicular eigenvectors.

32
00:03:52.500 --> 00:04:00.000
Once diagonal, powers are free: A to the k is P D to the k P inverse, one power per entry.

33
00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:07.500
Trap one: determinants do not add. The determinant of a sum is not the sum of determinants.

34
00:04:07.500 --> 00:04:15.000
Trap two: ranks do not add. The identity and its negative are both full rank; their sum is zero.

35
00:04:15.000 --> 00:04:22.500
Trap three: eigenvalues do not add either. A sum has its own eigenvalues.

36
00:04:22.500 --> 00:04:30.000
Trap four: row operations preserve rank but change the determinant.

37
00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:37.500
Step one: read the shape. It caps the rank and decides what "full rank" can mean.

38
00:04:37.500 --> 00:04:45.000
Step two: for a square matrix, the determinant or the pivots decide invertibility.

39
00:04:45.000 --> 00:04:52.500
Step three: compare ranks to decide the number of solutions, and count free variables as n minus rank.

40
00:04:52.500 --> 00:05:00.000
Step four: the characteristic polynomial, then check the answer against trace and determinant.

